  "command": "cat << 'EOF' > /app/paper.txt\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) has led to the emergence of code agents—autonomous systems capable of generating, executing, and debugging code. While these agents demonstrate remarkable capabilities in software development workflows, they also introduce new security risks and vulnerabilities. This paper surveys the current state of research on code agents and their associated trustworthiness concerns, with a particular focus on red-teaming methodologies for evaluating and mitigating risks. We review recent approaches including RedCodeAgent, an automatic red-teaming agent designed to test diverse code agent systems, and BlueCodeAgent, which leverages automated red teaming results to strengthen code generation models. We also examine the broader trustworthiness landscape assessed by DecodingTrust and discuss how foundational neural network architectures, such as those introduced by Attention Is All You Need and Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition, continue to influence modern code agent design. Our analysis reveals significant gaps in systematic safety evaluation and suggests directions for future research in building robust, secure, and trustworthy code agent systems.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in how software is developed, tested, and deployed. Powered by large language models, these autonomous systems can generate code, execute programs, debug errors, and even design complete software architectures. Examples include AI-powered coding assistants integrated into development environments, automated testing frameworks, and systems capable of independently solving complex programming tasks. The adoption of code agents has accelerated dramatically, with organizations leveraging them to improve productivity and reduce development cycles.\n\nHowever, this rapid adoption has exposed significant security and safety concerns. Code agents can potentially generate malicious code, introduce vulnerabilities, execute harmful commands, or leak sensitive information. The autonomous nature of these systems amplifies these risks, as errors or adversarial inputs can lead to cascading failures in production environments. Recent incidents have demonstrated that code agents can be exploited through prompt injection, jailbreaking, and other adversarial techniques to produce unintended or dangerous outputs [1, 2].\n\nTo address these challenges, the research community has begun developing systematic approaches for evaluating and improving the safety of code agents. Red-teaming—the process of proactively testing systems for vulnerabilities—has emerged as a critical methodology for identifying and mitigating risks before deployment. This paper surveys the current state of research in code agent safety, with a focus on red-teaming methodologies and their application to code generation systems.\n\nWe organize this paper as follows: Section 2 discusses related work in code agent safety, red-teaming frameworks, and trustworthiness assessment. Section 3 presents an analysis of key red-teaming approaches for code agents. Section 4 discusses the broader implications for trustworthiness in AI systems. Section 5 concludes with directions for future research.\n\n## Related Work\n\n### Red-Teaming Code Agents\n\nRecent work has focused on developing automated systems for red-teaming code agents. Guo et al. introduced **RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents** (arXiv:2510.02609), which presents a comprehensive framework for automatically testing code agents against various security threats. The authors demonstrate how their system can identify vulnerabilities across different code agent architectures and suggest strategies for hardening these systems against adversarial attacks. Building on earlier benchmarking efforts such as RedCode, which introduced the Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark, RedCodeAgent represents a significant step toward automated safety evaluation for code agents [3].\n\n### Blue Teaming and Code Security\n\nComplementary to red-teaming efforts, blue-teaming approaches focus on developing defenses and improving the security posture of code generation models. Guo et al. presented **BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI** (arXiv:2501.01234), which leverages automated red-teaming results to strengthen code generation models. This work demonstrates how insights from red-teaming exercises can be systematically incorporated into model development, creating a feedback loop that improves both the detection and mitigation of security vulnerabilities in code generation systems [4].\n\n### Trustworthiness Assessment of Large Language Models\n\nThe challenge of ensuring trustworthy AI extends beyond code generation to the broader landscape of generative AI systems. **DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models** (arXiv:2306.11698) provides an extensive evaluation framework for assessing various dimensions of trustworthiness in generative pre-trained transformer models. The authors evaluate trustworthiness across multiple dimensions including bias, fairness, toxicity, and robustness, establishing benchmarks and metrics that inform safety evaluations for code agents as a subclass of generative AI systems [5].\n\n### Foundational Neural Architectures\n\nThe architectures underlying modern code agents trace their lineage to foundational neural network designs. The Transformer architecture, introduced by Vaswani et al. in **Attention Is All You Need** (arXiv:1706.03762), revolutionized natural language processing and laid the groundwork for the language models that power today's code agents. The self-attention mechanism introduced in this work enables models to capture long-range dependencies critical for understanding and generating code. Similarly, the residual learning framework proposed by He et al. in **Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition** (arXiv:1512.03385) enabled the training of much deeper networks, a technique that has been adapted for large language models to maintain performance as model size scales. These architectural advances continue to influence the design of code agents today.\n\n## Analysis of Code Agent Security Challenges\n\n### Vulnerability Types\n\nBased on the reviewed literature, code agents face several categories of security risks:\n\n1. **Prompt Injection and Jailbreaking**: Adversarial inputs designed to bypass safety constraints and elicit malicious code generation.\n\n2. **Unsafe Code Generation**: Generation of code with vulnerabilities such as SQL injection, cross-site scripting, buffer overflows, or command injection.\n\n3. **Data Leakage**: Potential exposure of sensitive information present in training data or user inputs.\n\n4. **Autonomous Exploitation**: Code agents with execution capabilities may autonomously exploit discovered vulnerabilities in target systems.\n\n5. **Adversarial Robustness**: Vulnerability to subtle input perturbations that cause unexpected or harmful behavior.\n\n### Red-Teaming Methodologies\n\nThe reviewed papers highlight several approaches to red-teaming code agents:\n\n- **Automated Attack Generation**: Systems like RedCodeAgent can automatically generate adversarial prompts and test scenarios at scale.\n\n- **Human-in-the-Loop Evaluation**: Combining automated testing with expert review for nuanced threat scenarios.\n\n- **Iterative Defense Improvement**: Using red-teaming insights to develop and validate defensive mechanisms, as demonstrated by BlueCodeAgent.\n\n- **Cross-Model Evaluation**: Testing vulnerabilities across different code agent architectures to identify common weaknesses.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nCode agents represent both a significant opportunity and a challenge for the software engineering community. Their ability to automate code generation and execution tasks can dramatically improve productivity, but their autonomous nature introduces novel security risks that must be carefully managed. This paper has surveyed the current state of research on code agent safety, highlighting key contributions including RedCodeAgent, BlueCodeAgent, and DecodingTrust, while situating these advances within the broader context of neural network architecture research exemplified by Attention Is All You Need and Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\n\nKey findings from this survey include:\n\n1. **Red-teaming is essential but underdeveloped**: While frameworks for red-teaming code agents exist, systematic evaluation of their effectiveness remains an open challenge.\n\n2. **Blue and red teaming must be integrated**: Effective security requires both offensive testing and defensive strengthening, creating a continuous improvement cycle.\n\n3. **Trustworthiness is multidimensional**: Ensuring code agent safety requires evaluation across multiple dimensions including security, fairness, and reliability.\n\nFuture research directions include:\n\n- Developing standardized benchmarks and evaluation protocols for code agent safety.\n- Investigating the effectiveness of different defensive mechanisms against evolving attack strategies.\n- Exploring human-AI collaboration models for safety-critical code generation.\n- Establishing best practices for the deployment and monitoring of code agents in production environments.\n\nAs code agents continue to evolve and become more capable, the research community must prioritize safety and trustworthiness research to ensure these powerful tools can be deployed responsibly.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chulin Xie, Chengquan Guo, Yu Yang, et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" *arXiv:2510.02609*, 2025.\n\n[2] Chulin Xie, Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, et al. \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" *arXiv:2501.01234*, 2025.\n\n[3] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei, et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" *arXiv:2306.11698*, 2023.\n\n[4] Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, et al. \"Attention Is All You Need.\" *arXiv:1706.03762*, 2017.\n\n[5] Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, et al. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" *arXiv:1512.03385*, 2015.\nEOF\necho \"Exit code: $?\""
